WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.501
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:08.723
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.023 --> 00:00:16.772
Unit seventeen: Food Systems. In this unit, you will analyse how food is produced, traded and shared.

4
00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:19.272
food sovereignty.

5
00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:25.141
food sovereignty means the right of communities to control their own food production.

6
00:00:25.441 --> 00:00:28.253
Farmers' groups campaign for food sovereignty.

7
00:00:28.953 --> 00:00:31.942
Your turn. Say: food sovereignty.

8
00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:36.061
food sovereignty.

9
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.260
agribusiness.

10
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.955
agribusiness means large-scale commercial farming and food companies.

11
00:00:44.255 --> 00:00:47.717
Agribusiness controls much of the world's seed supply.

12
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:51.720
Your turn. Say: agribusiness.

13
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.688
agribusiness.

14
00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:57.882
monoculture.

15
00:00:58.582 --> 00:01:03.101
monoculture means growing only one crop over a large area.

16
00:01:03.401 --> 00:01:06.492
Monoculture makes crops vulnerable to disease.

17
00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:09.972
Your turn. Say: monoculture.

18
00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:13.800
monoculture.

19
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:15.980
food miles.

20
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.410
food miles means the distance food travels from farm to plate.

21
00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:23.150
Imported apples have high food miles.

22
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.095
Your turn. Say: food miles.

23
00:01:29.795 --> 00:01:30.889
food miles.

24
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:34.196
supply chain disruption.

25
00:01:34.896 --> 00:01:40.704
supply chain disruption means interruption to the flow of goods from producers to consumers.

26
00:01:41.004 --> 00:01:44.048
The pandemic caused major supply chain disruption.

27
00:01:44.748 --> 00:01:48.364
Your turn. Say: supply chain disruption.

28
00:01:51.064 --> 00:01:52.971
supply chain disruption.

29
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:55.577
smallholder farmer.

30
00:01:56.277 --> 00:02:00.401
smallholder farmer means a farmer with a small piece of land.

31
00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:04.244
Most Cambodian rice is grown by smallholder farmers.

32
00:02:04.944 --> 00:02:09.141
Your turn. Say: smallholder farmer.

33
00:02:11.841 --> 00:02:13.318
smallholder farmer.

34
00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:15.701
food insecurity.

35
00:02:16.401 --> 00:02:20.920
food insecurity means not having reliable access to enough nutritious food.

36
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:25.100
Food insecurity rises when prices suddenly increase.

37
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.695
Your turn. Say: food insecurity.

38
00:02:32.395 --> 00:02:33.849
food insecurity.

39
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.211
lab-grown meat.

40
00:02:36.911 --> 00:02:41.279
lab-grown meat means meat produced from animal cells in a laboratory.

41
00:02:41.579 --> 00:02:44.531
Lab-grown meat could reduce emissions from farming.

42
00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:49.160
Your turn. Say: lab-grown meat.

43
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.163
lab-grown meat.

44
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.440
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.877
Rithy, where does the rice in your local market come from?

46
00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:05.308
Mostly from smallholder farmers in Takeo and Battambang.

47
00:03:06.617 --> 00:03:11.498
That's good for food miles. But agribusiness is expanding fast here, isn't it?

48
00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:17.871
Yes, and some companies are pushing monoculture, which raises serious concerns.

49
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:24.216
It certainly poses risks. One disease can wipe out an entire harvest.

50
00:03:25.517 --> 00:03:29.769
The pandemic also showed how fragile global supply chains are.

51
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:37.381
Indeed. Supply chain disruption caused a sharp rise in food insecurity worldwide.

52
00:03:38.683 --> 00:03:41.554
That's why food sovereignty makes so much sense to me.

53
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:45.384
Grammar focus: Collocations.

54
00:03:45.684 --> 00:03:48.891
Collocations are words that naturally go together.

55
00:03:49.191 --> 00:03:55.985
We say pose a risk, raise concerns and a sharp rise, not make a risk or a fast rise.

56
00:03:56.285 --> 00:03:59.550
Learning collocations makes your English sound natural.

57
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:05.112
Learning words in natural combinations makes your English sound fluent and precise.

58
00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:09.141
Now read and listen. Who Feeds the World?.

59
00:04:09.441 --> 00:04:13.332
Smallholder farmers produce around a third of the world's food.

60
00:04:14.617 --> 00:04:17.917
Yet many of them face food insecurity themselves.

61
00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:25.036
Over recent decades, agribusiness has grown rapidly, often promoting monoculture.

62
00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:33.493
This model can increase yields, but it also poses risks to biodiversity and soil health.

63
00:04:34.783 --> 00:04:41.427
Global supply chains deliver cheap food, but disruptions can cause sharp rises in prices.

64
00:04:42.717 --> 00:04:48.745
Supporters of food sovereignty argue that communities should control their own food systems.

65
00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:56.717
New technologies, such as lab-grown meat, may reduce the environmental impact of farming.

66
00:04:58.017 --> 00:05:02.698
The challenge is to meet growing demand without harming people or the planet.

67
00:05:03.983 --> 00:05:06.752
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:07.052 --> 00:05:10.294
Growing only one crop over a large area is blank.

69
00:05:10.594 --> 00:05:15.559
A, monoculture. B, food miles. C, agribusiness.

70
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.921
The answer is A: monoculture.

71
00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:26.310
The new policy blank serious concerns.

72
00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.020
A, makes. B, raises. C, does.

73
00:05:34.017 --> 00:05:36.724
The answer is B: raises.

74
00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:40.669
There has been a blank rise in rice prices.

75
00:05:40.969 --> 00:05:45.145
A, sharp. B, strong. C, high.

76
00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.614
The answer is A: sharp.

77
00:05:53.117 --> 00:05:56.115
Not having reliable access to enough food is blank.

78
00:05:56.415 --> 00:06:03.238
A, food sovereignty. B, food insecurity. C, smallholder farmer.

79
00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:10.585
The answer is B: food insecurity.

80
00:06:12.083 --> 00:06:17.287
According to the text, smallholder farmers produce about blank of the world's food.

81
00:06:17.587 --> 00:06:22.553
A, a tenth. B, a third. C, a half.

82
00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.759
The answer is B: a third.

83
00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:36.467
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can Cambodia ensure that everyone has enough healthy food?

84
00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:48.190
Part two: Use at least three collocations: propose two solutions and explain one risk.

85
00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:08.696
Firstly, the government should support smallholder farmers to meet local demand. Secondly, diverse crops would reduce reliance on imports. However, heavy reliance on monoculture still poses serious risks to soil and income.

86
00:07:08.996 --> 00:07:10.764
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:15.575
Great work today! You learned eight new words and collocations.

88
00:07:15.875 --> 00:07:20.420
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.465
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:23.765 --> 00:07:26.649
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

